    "result_text": "Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number-length validation, single-sourced in the shared `toE164` normalizer so both the create request and the uniqueness check use the same definition.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n- `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` \u2014 after the generic 8\u201315 digit check, a new `checkNationalLength` step inspects the country code and enforces its required national-number length: `+61` \u21d2 9 national digits, `+1` \u21d2 10. A wrong length throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (already mapped to **400** by `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`). Unlisted country codes fall back to the generic 8\u201315 rule, preserving prior behavior.\n- Updated the class/method Javadoc and `InvalidTelephoneException`'s Javadoc to document the new rule.\n\nBecause `toE164` is reused by `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` (which already skips existing owners whose value can't form valid E.164), no other call site needed changes. The given test \u2014 `+61 123` \u2192 400 \u2014 passes, and so does a genuinely wrong-length-but-in-range case like `+61 1234567890` (10 national digits, now rejected where it previously slipped through).",
